"""A caption is cut where its printed caption ends, and joined only to the lines that continue it.

Four faults of f1 (an Ind AS filing, standalone, INR lakhs), each seen in what the model received:

* NUMBERS INSIDE A CAPTION READ AS FIGURES. Note 36 prints its dividends as sentences — "Interim
  dividend for the year ended 31 March 2025 of ₹ 1.50" / "per share (previous year: nil)  273.60".
  The per-share rate became the row's current figure, and the date inside "(previous year: … ended
  31 March 2024)" filled two figure slots ahead of the real ones: {current: 31, prior: 2024, col2:
  364.80, col3: 547.20}. A rate tight after "₹" and a date inside an open bracket are the caption's.
* A CAPTION'S DASH READ AS A COLUMN. Note 22's ageing schedule captions "(iii) Disputed dues -
  MSME"; the dash made a column in the label area and "(i) MSME", "(ii) Others" reached the model
  as "(i)" and "(ii)".
* A MOVEMENT SCHEDULE'S BLOCK HEADING JOINED ONTO ITS FIRST ROW. "Gross carrying amount" /
  "Accumulated amortisation" / "Net carrying amount" are printed flush with the rows under them,
  and the wrap merge read "Gross carrying amount Balance as at 1 April 2023" as one caption. The
  heading is the rows' group.
* A NUMBERED FACE HEADING JOINED ONTO THE INDENTED ROWS UNDER IT. "X. Other comprehensive income"
  / "A. (i) Items that will not be reclassified to profit or loss" / "Remeasurements of the defined
  benefit plans" were one caption. The roman-numbered heading is the rows' section, the lettered
  one their sub-heading.

The word geometry below is f1's own, read from the PDF.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import importlib.util
import sys
from pathlib import Path

from app.core.models.geometry import BBox
from app.services.row_reconstruct import (
    Word, _heads_a_movement_block, _heads_numbered_face_block, _matrix_cells, _scan_row,
    build_line_items)

H = 0.009


def _words(rows):
    return [Word(text=t, bbox=BBox(x0=x0, y0=y, x1=x1, y1=y + H)) for t, x0, x1, y in rows]


def _build(rows, *, on_face=False, statement=None):
    items, _ = build_line_items(_words(rows), page_index=1, document_id="d", source_kind="native",
                                on_face=on_face, statement=statement)
    return items


def _figures(li):
    return {ev.period_label: str(ev.value) for ev in li.values.values()}


